<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Purdue campus world for the delivery mission.

  Derived from navigation-stack/Path_Planning/worlds/Purdue_map.world, but the
  campus mesh itself is NOT loaded. Read the next section before adding it
  back.

  THE 933 MB CAMPUS MESH IS NOT USABLE IN THIS SITL SETUP

  Purdue_map.world loads Purdue_map.dae as both visual and collision geometry.
  Measured here, on a 16-core / 14 GB machine:

    as collision  gzserver pegged one core for 11 minutes building the ODE
                  trimesh BVH without finishing, at 2.9 GB RSS and climbing.
                  ODE trimesh collision against a mesh that size cannot step
                  in real time anyway, and PX4 runs in lockstep with the
                  simulator, so the whole flight would crawl with it.

    as visual     the world loads in ~100 s and steps at RTF 0.83, but the
                  simulation is starved enough that the GPS plugin never
                  delivers a fix: EKF2 reports a horizontal accuracy in the
                  kilometres, local_position_invalid and
                  global_position_invalid stay true, and the aircraft never
                  passes preflight. Removing the mesh and changing nothing
                  else makes position valid within ~60 s of sim time.

  So the mesh is out entirely. What is kept is what the mission actually needs
  from "the Purdue world": the campus geodetic origin, so GPS coordinates and
  the delivery waypoint are real West Lafayette positions, and real obstacles
  in the transit corridor.

  If you want the campus geometry back, decimate it first — target something
  on the order of 10^4 triangles for collision and 10^5 for visual — and
  re-test that GPS still converges before flying anything against it.

  What this world contains:

  1. A geodetic origin at the landing pad (40.4237, -86.9212, 187 m).
  2. An AprilTag landing pad at the local origin. The mission always returns
     home and lands on the tag to charge; without it SEARCH finds nothing and
     every run ends in a failsafe land.
  3. PX4's stock physics block. Purdue_map.world declares no <physics> at all,
     and apriltag_landing.world's hand-written block used a 10 ms step with no
     ODE solver tuning — coarse enough that the contact solver under-resolves
     the airframe on its legs, the simulated accelerometer reads low, and EKF2
     reports "Preflight Fail: High Accelerometer Bias".
  4. Two box obstacles straddling the transit corridor, tall enough to sit
     inside the costmap's 2..40 m band, so Nav2 has something real to plan
     around and obstacle avoidance is genuinely exercised.

  Run:
    PX4_SITL_WORLD=purdue_campus make px4_sitl gazebo-classic_typhoon_h480

  Delivery waypoint used with this world (about 108 m out, bearing ~22 deg,
  with both boxes across the straight line to it):
    delivery_lat:=40.4245982  delivery_lon:=-86.9207278
-->
<sdf version="1.6">
  <world name="purdue_campus">

    <!-- Geodetic origin for the GPS plugin. Without this the sensor publishes
         in Gazebo but PX4 never gets a fix, the EKF reports a horizontal
         accuracy in the kilometres, and preflight blocks on
         local/global_position_invalid. Set to the landing pad. -->
    <spherical_coordinates>
      <surface_model>EARTH_WGS84</surface_model>
      <latitude_deg>40.4289204</latitude_deg>
      <longitude_deg>-86.9279914</longitude_deg>
      <elevation>187</elevation>
      <heading_deg>0</heading_deg>
    </spherical_coordinates>

    <!-- PX4's stock SITL physics. Do not coarsen: see the header. -->
    <physics name='default_physics' default='0' type='ode'>
      <gravity>0 0 -9.8066</gravity>
      <ode>
        <solver>
          <type>quick</type>
          <iters>10</iters>
          <sor>1.3</sor>
          <use_dynamic_moi_rescaling>0</use_dynamic_moi_rescaling>
        </solver>
        <constraints>
          <cfm>0</cfm>
          <erp>0.2</erp>
          <contact_max_correcting_vel>100</contact_max_correcting_vel>
          <contact_surface_layer>0.001</contact_surface_layer>
        </constraints>
      </ode>
      <max_step_size>0.004</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1</real_time_factor>
      <real_time_update_rate>250</real_time_update_rate>
      <magnetic_field>6.0e-6 2.3e-5 -4.2e-5</magnetic_field>
    </physics>

    <include>
      <uri>model://ground_plane</uri>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://sun</uri>
    </include>

    <!-- Landing pad. The aircraft only ever lands here, to charge. -->
    <model name="Apriltag_36_11_00000">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0 0 0.01 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry>
            <box><size>0.5 0.5 0.001</size></box>
          </geometry>
          <material>
            <script>
              <uri>model://Apriltag36_11_00000/materials/scripts</uri>
              <uri>model://Apriltag36_11_00000/materials/textures</uri>
              <name>Apriltag36_11_00000</name>
            </script>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Campus mesh: VISUAL ONLY. See the header for why there is no
         collision copy of this. -->
    <!-- The campus mesh is deliberately absent. See the header. -->


    <!-- Two obstacles straddling the transit corridor from the pad at (0,0)
         to Krach Lawn, 596 m away. Gazebo x is local NORTH, y is local EAST,
         so these sit at roughly one third and two thirds along the route.

         They are DIFFERENT HEIGHTS on purpose, and that is the test:

           building_tall  25 m — ABOVE the 15 m transit altitude. The aircraft
                                 must route around it. If it flies through,
                                 obstacle avoidance is not working.
           building_low    8 m — BELOW the transit altitude. The aircraft
                                 should fly straight over it without detouring.
                                 Before the costmap became altitude-aware this
                                 was marked exactly like the tall one, and the
                                 aircraft detoured around something it clears
                                 by 7 m. -->
    <!-- THE REAL PURDUE CAMPUS, from the OSM extract in this repo
         (Path_Planning/worlds/map-2.osm). 26 real buildings within 480 m
         of the pad: each polygon is the true OSM footprint, converted from
         lat/lon and extruded to height, with collision geometry.

         AXES: Gazebo's world frame here is ENU — +x is EAST, +y is NORTH.
         Points below are therefore written (east north).

         This is not a detail. The first version of this file was written
         (north east), which transposed the whole campus across the diagonal.
         The aircraft still flew the correct GPS route, because that is
         computed in PX4's own frame and owes nothing to this file — so every
         building simply sat somewhere the aircraft never went, the lidar saw
         nothing, and the flights looked clean while proving nothing about
         obstacle avoidance.

         Verified empirically rather than assumed: moving the model to
         gazebo x=+60 moved its LONGITUDE 60 m east and left latitude
         unchanged.

         These are extruded footprints, not the 933 MB campus mesh — that mesh
         never finishes loading as collision and starves GPS even as scenery.

         HEIGHTS: OSM `height` where present, else `building:levels` x 3.5 m,
         else assumed 20 m. Each model records which. The assumed ones are a
         guess.
    -->
    <!-- Hillenbrand Hall — centre NED (-249, 113), height 28 m (osm-levels) -->
